e screen I would recommend using a  stack this will allow us to set the map  on the bottom and then we can overlay  different controls as needed so we can  start by adding the google map widget as  the first widget in the stack it  requires an initial camera position  which is a latitude and longitude point  for the actual map to be centered on we  pass the coordinates into the target  option and you'll also want to set the  zoom level here as well at this point if  you run the app you should have a  full-screen map that looks similar to  this now the map itself has its own life  cycle hook called on map created we'll  set up a handler for that life cycle  event and the important thing that we do  here is set up the controller on this  widget the map controller is super  important because it allows you to  interact with the map by setting markers  on it zooming to a different position as  well as a bunch of other things so those  are the most important setup options  that you'll have for your map but you  can also do other things here too like  if you want to track the user's location  you'll set my location enabled to true  you can change the map type for example  if you want to show the satellite  imagery and the last thing we'll do for  this map is enable camera tracking so we  can always get the current center point  of the camera now that we have a basic  map set up let's add a control to it  we'll add a button to the bottom right  that can add a new marker based on the  cameras current position  we'll go back up to our stack in the  widget tree and add a new positioned  widget we can define the bottom and  right properties to put it in the bottom  right corner and then we'll define its  child as a flat button that has an icon  and when this button is pressed it will  trigger the add marker event handler now  we need to actually define this method  the first thing we'll do inside of it is  define the marker itself the first thing  we'll need to give it is a position and  we can do that by looking at the map  controllers camera position and that  will give us a latitude and longitude to  place the marker itself on the map we  can also customize the icon here and we  can also add information to the info  window text and that will be displayed  when the user clicks on the marker and  finally to add this marker to the map we  just called the map controller add  marker with this marker options object  now we should be able to pan around the  map and when we click the button it  should add a marker to the center the  next thing we'll do is use the flutter  location plug-in to track the users  actual position this is very easy and we  can get the user's location either as a  future or as a stream we'll go up here  to the top of our stateful widget and  set up a new instance of location now  we'll write a method called animate to  user that will animate the camera to the  users current location  this will be an async function and then  we can get the location by just calling  a wait location get location and you can  use on location change if you want to  listen to it as a real-time stream now  let's animate the camera to this users  position for that we can use the map  controller call animate camera and then  we'll update it with a new camera  position now we have a way to  programmatically control where the  camera is animated to and you can see  our current position is this little blue  dot in the middle of the screen at this  point we have a map the user's location  and the next phase is to save locations  in the firestore database you can see  here we have a locations collection and  you'll notice that every document in  this collection has a position field  that has a geo hash and a geo point geo  flutter fire will create this entire  data structure for you including the geo  hash value I'm not going to go into how  geohashes work in this video but it's  basically just an algorithm that allows  you to encode a bounding box on the  surface of a sphere in this case we're  talking about planet Earth and this is  important because we need this string to  be able to query firestore for  everything within a certain radius now  we'll go back to the top of our  application and import the remaining  dependencies  then notice in the stateful widget I'm  setting up a value for fire store and  also for the Geo Fire client before we  can query anything we first need to save  some geolocation data in the database  for that I'm creating an ad geo point  method and this is going to add a  document to firestorm which will always  return a future of a document reference  the actual value for the Geo point will  be the users current location so first  we'll get the location with our location  service and then we'll set up a geo  point based on that latitude and  longitude a Geo Fire point is just a  special data structure that's primarily  responsible for calculating that geo  hash but can also do other things like  calculate distances between points the  next step is to make a reference to the  collection that has our geolocation data  and then we'll define the position as  the point data and that's really all it  takes to add a geo point to firestore  for the users current location let's go  up to our button and replace the add  marker with the add geo point method you  can see I have fire store set up here  with an empty database and then as I  click this button it's adding new  documents to the database in real time  currently you'll notice that all these  geo points are the same that's because  it's based on the device location which  currently isn't changing but here's a  little tip if you're working on Android  specifically you can go into the  emulator settings and send your own  custom GPS locations to the device when  you push then you'll see the blue dot  move around the screen to the new  location and if we add another geo point  this time you'll see it's a different  geo hash and a different set of  coordinates now that we have a way to  get data into the database  let's query it and then add markers to  the map reactively the query itself is  based on a radius and we want to be able  to change that radius so we're defining  it as an RX behavior subject which is  basically an observable that we can push  new values to and always has the concept  of a current value it's an extremely  powerful data structure for dealing with  state management both on the web and in  flutter' the next value is our actual  geo query which is a stream of the  documents from the firestore database  and then we'll set up a another property  for the subscription this is definitely  the type of subscription that can result  in a memory leak so you'll want to make  sure to cancel it when this widget is  disposed now we'll go into our stack and  we'll add a slider so the user has a way  to actually change the radius value the  slider widget is built into flutter  material and it's pretty straightforward  we set a min and Max value and then  divide that by a certain number of set  we can always grab the current value  from our behavior subject by just  calling value on it so whenever set  state is called on the stateful widget  it will get the current value from that  radius subject and whenever the user  slides it will trigger this on changed  event which we can then use to update  the query but the query is the very last  thing I want to show you first I want to  show you what we do once we have the  actual documents from the database  anytime there's a change firestore will  emit the entire list of documents so we  can loop over them and update the  markers on the map this marker code is  almost identical to what I showed you  earlier the only difference is we're  taking the geolocation data from the  firestore document and using that for  the position of the marker and also geo  flutter fire will give us the actual  distance of this position from the query  center point we can use that data as the  info window text to show the user how  far they are from a given point in  kilometers the next step is to set up  the initial query and start listening to  it we'll start by getting the users  current location and then we'll make a  reference to the locations data and fire  store to make this query we need to  start from a Centerpoint four that will  set up a geo fire point based on this  user's location now our actual  subscription is dependent on two  different streams first we need to  listen to the radius value and then we  want to use that to query the database  and listen to the value of those  documents in real-time this is where I  really like to use rx start because we  can call switch map on the radius and  then switch to an observable of the  documents in the database and to make  that query we just call the geo fire  collection within and then we pass in  the center point the radius that we want  to search and then the property of the  document that contains the geo location  data and I also recommend using strict  mode here which will ensure that only  points within the radius are included in  the query otherwise you might get some  additional documents in the query that  go beyond the radius because of the way  geohashes are square-shaped  and lastly we called listen on the  stream and then we'll run our update  markers method that we defined earlier  the next step is to update the query  when the user slides the slider around  now just to make this a little more  polished I created a map of different  zoom values so we can zoom out when the  user goes to a higher radius that part  is optional but the important thing is  that you call set state here and then  add a new value to the radius behavior  subject so we already have that set up  on our slider the last thing we have to  do is call start query  when our map is created and it's also  probably a good idea to come down here  and override the dispose method and  cancel that 